@subsubsection Verification

The verifier and the collectors check the ownership invariant
after the fact, for what the barrier does not see. Each violation is
a list @code{(source-object slot-address target-address origin store-pc)}:
ORIGIN is @code{:collection} for a pointer a collection or verification
found, @code{:recorded-store} for a store the barrier recorded and let
proceed, and @code{:signaled-store} for a store it signaled
@code{sb-fiber:heap-store-error} for, which left the object unchanged unless
a handler continued the store. For a store, SLOT-ADDRESS is 0 and
STORE-PC is the return address into the code that made it, which
@code{sb-fiber:store-site} names; for a collection STORE-PC is @code{nil}.

@anchor{Function sb-fiber verify-heap}
@ffindex @sortas{verify-heap sb-fiber} verify-heap [sb-fiber]
@deffn{Function} sb-fiber:verify-heap &optional heap
Check every pointer held by @code{heap}'s objects against the ownership
rules.  Returns a list of (SOURCE-OBJECT SLOT-ADDRESS TARGET-ADDRESS
ORIGIN STORE-PC @code{kind}), as @code{take-heap-violations} describes, for each pointer
into another local heap or a freed page.  @code{heap} must be installed on the
current thread.
@end deffn
@anchor{Function sb-fiber verify-all-heaps}
@ffindex @sortas{verify-all-heaps sb-fiber} verify-all-heaps [sb-fiber]
@deffn{Function} sb-fiber:verify-all-heaps
Run a full global collection with reference checking enabled and
return the ownership violations it found: pointers from the global heap
into a local heap, and pointers between different local heaps.
@end deffn
@anchor{Function sb-fiber heap-reference-checking}
@ffindex @sortas{heap-reference-checking sb-fiber} heap-reference-checking [sb-fiber]
@deffn{Function} sb-fiber:heap-reference-checking
True if garbage collections record ownership violations.
@end deffn
@anchor{Function sb-fiber heap-violations}
@ffindex @sortas{heap-violations sb-fiber} heap-violations [sb-fiber]
@deffn{Function} sb-fiber:heap-violations
The ownership violations recorded by collections and the store
barrier since the last reset, as a list of (SOURCE-OBJECT SLOT-ADDRESS
TARGET-ADDRESS ORIGIN STORE-PC @code{kind}), as @code{take-heap-violations} describes.
@end deffn
@anchor{Function sb-fiber reset-heap-violations}
@ffindex @sortas{reset-heap-violations sb-fiber} reset-heap-violations [sb-fiber]
@deffn{Function} sb-fiber:reset-heap-violations
@end deffn
@anchor{Function sb-fiber take-heap-violations}
@ffindex @sortas{take-heap-violations sb-fiber} take-heap-violations [sb-fiber]
@deffn{Function} sb-fiber:take-heap-violations
Remove and return the ownership violations recorded since the last
call: a list of (SOURCE-OBJECT SLOT-ADDRESS TARGET-ADDRESS ORIGIN
STORE-PC @code{kind}), and as a second value the number noted, which is exact and
may exceed the length of the list -- a burst larger than the record keeps
its first entries.

ORIGIN tells a pointer that exists from a store that was stopped:
@code{:collection} for a pointer a collection or verification found,
@code{:recorded-store} for a store the barrier recorded and let proceed under
@code{:check-stores} @code{:record}, and @code{:signaled-store} for a store the barrier
signaled @code{heap-store-error} for under @code{:check-stores} @code{:error}, which left the
object as it was unless a handler chose @code{continue}.  For a store,
SLOT-ADDRESS is 0 and STORE-PC is the return address into the code that
made it, which @code{store-site} names; for a collection STORE-PC is @code{nil}.

@code{kind} is how the barrier classified a store, as @code{heap-store-error-kind}
names it: @code{:escape} for a pointer into a local heap stored into a global
object, @code{:cross-heap} for one stored into another heap's object, and
@code{:global} for a strict heap's store into a global object of a value that
is not an escape.  It is @code{nil} for a collection.  It is recorded at the
store because it cannot be recovered after the fact: the heap that owned
an escaped value may have been released by the time the record is read.

Clearing the record and copying it out happen in one step.  Violations
are noted from collections and from the store barrier on any thread,
without a lock, so one noted between a @code{heap-violations} call and a
@code{reset-heap-violations} call is lost: neither call sees it and the reset
discards it.  Use this wherever violations are drained while other
threads are running.
@end deffn
@anchor{Function sb-fiber store-site}
@ffindex @sortas{store-site sb-fiber} store-site [sb-fiber]
@deffn{Function} sb-fiber:store-site pc
The name of the function whose code contains @code{pc}, the STORE-PC of a
store violation, or @code{nil} when no code object holds @code{pc}.  The runtime keeps
the address rather than the code object, so the code can have been
collected by the time this is asked; the name is that of whatever code
holds @code{pc} then.
@end deffn
@node sb fiber heap layers
@subsubsection Building a process layer

@code{sb-fiber} stops at the primitives. A process layer in the manner
of Erlang/OTP, with carriers, mailboxes with selective receive,
links, monitors, exit signals, a registry and timers, is a library
outside SBCL, and everything it needs is exported here:
@code{sb-fiber:make-fiber} with @code{:heap} for a process that owns its memory,
@code{sb-fiber:send-message} and @code{sb-fiber:receive-message} for a copy in and
an adoption out, @code{sb-fiber:heap-mailbox-count} as a wake condition a
carrier can read from outside the heap, @code{sb-fiber:interrupt-fiber} for
an exit signal delivered at the next resume, @code{sb-fiber:without-heap}
for bookkeeping the layer keeps in global structures, and
@code{sb-fiber:release-fiber} to return a process's memory in one step. The
test file @code{tests/fiber-carriers.impure.lisp} is the least such layer,
a pool of carrier threads, and exercises those primitives with the
sender and the receiver on different threads.

